<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nzinga a Nkuwu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">converted <a href="/years/1491">1491</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nzinga a Nkuwu was a king, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manikongo">manikongo</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> in the late fifteenth century. He received Portuguese envoys and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1491, taking the name João I. His conversion began a new ideological framework for royal governance in the Kongo Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nzinga-a-nkuwu.jpg" alt="Nzinga a Nkuwu" width="254" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Duflos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Roy_de_Congo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The baptism of King <b>Nzinga a Nkuwu</b> as João I in 1491 marked the beginning of a new ideological framework for royal government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
